       "  '8c99cc9b-637c-4dc8-8b45-e816a19def1b'],\n",
       " 'embeddings': None,\n",
       " 'documents': ['Northbridge Bank plc - SME Credit Policy\\nFICTIONAL DOCUMENT. Northbridge Bank plc does not exist. This policy, and every threshold, limit, scorecard weight, PD, LGD, authority level and worked example in it, is invented for educational and portfolio-project purposes. It is not, and must not be presented as, the policy of any real institution.',\n",
       "  '6. Eligibility\\nAn application that fails a mandatory eligibility criterion must be declined or approved as a Policy Exception; it may not proceed to credit assessment as if eligible. Eligibility is tested by the decision engine for engine-routed applications and by the RM (checked by Credit) for all others.',\n",
       "  \"6.1 Mandatory eligibility criteria\\n| - |\\n| Registration, Requirement = UK-registered company, LLP or partnership, or UK-resident sole trader, trading principally in the UK. Registration, Exception route = None for non-UK obligors (Corporate & International). Trading history, Requirement = ≥ 24 months for standard policy; < 24 months → Start-up policy (Section 31). Trading history, Exception route = Start-up policy. Filing status, Requirement = Companies House filings up to date (no overdue accounts or confirmation statement > 30 days late). Filing status, Exception route = Credit Manager if late filing explained and cured by CP. Minimum turnover, Requirement = ≥ £50k for term lending; none for card/overdraft ≤ £10k. Minimum turnover, Exception route = Credit Manager. Ownership transparency, Requirement = All persons with ≥ 25% ownership or significant control identified and verified; ultimate beneficial owners (UBOs) verified to 10% where higher-risk indicators exist. Ownership transparency, Exception route = None. Management, Requirement = At least one director/partner with ≥ 2 years' relevant sector experience. Management, Exception route = Credit Manager. Sector, Requirement = Not Prohibited; Restricted sectors meet sector conditions (6.3). Sector, Exception route = Restricted: per 6.3. Prohibited: none. Credit history, Requirement = No unsatisfied CCJs > £5k in last 24 months (business or principals); no insolvency of principals in last 6 years; no IVA in last 3 years. Credit history, Exception route = Senior Credit Manager if explained and satisfied. Account conduct, Requirement = Where banked with Northbridge ≥ 6 months: no returned items in last 3 months; no unarranged excess > 30 days in last 6 months. Account conduct, Exception route = Credit Manager. Tax, Requirement = No known HMRC arrears outside an agreed Time to Pay arrangement; Time to\",\n",
       "  \"6.1 Mandatory eligibility criteria\\n| - |\\n Pay arrangements disclosed and being met. Tax, Exception route = Credit Manager if TTP being met |\\n\\nFinancial information, Requirement = Information required by Section 8 provided. Financial information, Exception route = None (incomplete applications are not assessed). Purpose, Requirement = Legal, clearly identified, consistent with the business model and not to repay HMRC arrears, other lenders' arrears or fund distributions to shareholders. Purpose, Exception route = Refinancing of arrears only under Section 26. Source of funds, Requirement = Equity contributions and deposits supporting the application are from an evidenced, legitimate source. Source of funds, Exception route = None. Existing relationship, Requirement = Not required. Non-customers must open a Northbridge operating account before drawdown for any facility > £250k. Existing relationship, Exception route = Credit Manager\",\n",
       "  '6.2 Sector appetite classification\\nNorthbridge classifies sectors by SIC 2007 code into four appetite categories, refreshed semi-annually by Portfolio Credit Risk based on default rates, forward-looking economic indicators and concentration.\\nAcceptable - Growth, Meaning = Sectors where Northbridge seeks to grow share. Acceptable - Growth, Approval impact = Standard policy. Acceptable - Neutral, Meaning = Standard appetite. Acceptable - Neutral, Approval impact = Standard policy. Restricted, Meaning = Elevated credit, legal, regulatory, reputational or financial-crime risk; lending only on enhanced conditions. Restricted, Approval impact = Sector conditions + one authority level higher (Section 19). Prohibited, Meaning = Outside appetite. Prohibited, Approval impact = Decline; no exception route',\n",
       "  '6.3 Sector classification (illustrative)\\nHealthcare (GP, dental, care with CQC rating Good/Outstanding), Category = Acceptable - Growth. Healthcare (GP, dental, care with CQC rating Good/Outstanding), Rationale and conditions = Contracted public income; regulated quality. Care homes rated Requires Improvement → Restricted. Professional services, Category = Acceptable - Growth. Professional services, Rationale and conditions = Stable fee income; low capital intensity. Manufacturing (excl. defence- adjacent), Category = Acceptable - Growth. Manufacturing (excl. defence- adjacent), Rationale and conditions = Tangible security; strong UK supply- chain policy support. Technology (revenue-generating, positive EBITDA), Category = Acceptable - Neutral. Technology (revenue-generating, positive EBITDA), Rationale and conditions = Cash-flow lending only; pre-revenue → Restricted (venture debt team). Wholesale and distribution, Category = Acceptable - Neutral. Wholesale and distribution, Rationale and conditions = Thin margins; working-capital intensive. Agriculture, Category = Acceptable - Neutral. Agriculture, Rationale and conditions = Land security; subsidy-transition and weather risk. Transport and logistics, Category = Acceptable - Neutral. Transport and logistics, Rationale and conditions = Fuel and labour sensitivity; asset- backed. Retail (non-food), Category = Acceptable - Neutral. Retail (non-food), Rationale and conditions = Structural shift online; lease liabilities included in leverage',\n",
       "  '6.3 Sector classification (illustrative)\\n| - |\\n| Hospitality (pubs, restaurants, hotels), Category = Restricted. Hospitality (pubs, restaurants, hotels), Rationale and conditions = High default rates; energy, labour and consumer-demand sensitivity. Max senior leverage 2.5x; DSCR ≥ 1.35x; trading ≥ 3 years. Construction - main contractors < £5m turnover, Category = Restricted. Construction - main contractors < £5m turnover, Rationale and conditions = Fixed-price contract risk; retentions; payment-chain insolvency contagion. Bonding exposure counted in full. Recruitment and staffing, Category = Restricted. Recruitment and staffing, Rationale and conditions = Thin margins; HMRC/IR35 and umbrella-company compliance; PAYE arrears risk. Invoice finance preferred. Commercial property development, Category = Restricted. Commercial property development, Rationale and conditions = Section 32 conditions. Energy - oil & gas services, Category = Restricted. Energy - oil & gas services, Rationale and conditions = Transition risk; Climate Risk Standard sector review required. Cash-intensive businesses (car washes, nail bars, takeaways, vape shops), Category = Restricted. Cash-intensive businesses (car washes, nail bars, takeaways, vape shops), Rationale and conditions = Heightened money-laundering risk. Enhanced Due Diligence; cash- turnover reconciliation to bank statements. Gambling (licensed), Category = Restricted. Gambling (licensed), Rationale and conditions = Reputational and AML; UK Gambling Commission licence required and in good standing. Cryptoasset businesses, Category = Restricted. Cryptoasset businesses, Rationale and conditions = FCA cryptoasset registration mandatory; Financial Crime Risk approval. Defence manufacturers, Category = Restricted. Defence manufacturers, Rationale and conditions = Export-control and reputational review; no controversial weapons. Adult entertainment, Category',\n",
       "  '6.3 Sector classification (illustrative)\\n| - |\\n = Prohibited. Adult entertainment, Rationale and conditions = Reputational risk outside appetite. Unlicensed money service businesses / unregistered payment institutions, Category = Prohibited. Unlicensed money service businesses / unregistered payment institutions, Rationale and conditions = Regulatory and AML. Controversial weapons (cluster munitions, anti-personnel mines, chemical/biological), Category = Prohibited. Controversial weapons (cluster munitions, anti-personnel mines, chemical/biological), Rationale and conditions = Legal and reputational. Payday / high-cost short-term lenders, Category = Prohibited. Payday / high-cost short-term lenders, Rationale and conditions = Conduct and reputational. Thermal coal mining or new coal- fired generation, Category = Prohibited. Thermal coal mining or new coal- fired generation, Rationale and conditions = Climate Risk Standard. Businesses whose principal activity is unlawful in any UK jurisdiction, Category = Prohibited. Businesses whose principal activity is unlawful in any UK jurisdiction, Rationale and conditions = Legal. Shell companies with no trading activity (other than SPVs within Section 32 property lending), Category = Prohibited. Shell companies with no trading activity (other than SPVs within Section 32 property lending), Rationale and conditions = Fraud and AML |\\n',\n",
       "  '6.4 Geographic considerations\\n- Lending is available across England, Scotland, Wales and Northern Ireland. Security documentation must follow the law of the jurisdiction where the asset sits (for example, standard securities and floating charges under Scots law; mortgages registered with Land Registry NI).\\n- Obligors with operations or key customers in sanctioned or high-risk third countries require Financial Crime Risk sign-off before credit assessment.\\n- Property in Flood Zone 3 (or SEPA/NRW/NI equivalents) requires the climate overlay in Section 37.',\n",
       "  '6.5 Business model tests\\nThe RM must be able to state, in three sentences or fewer, how the business makes money, who pays it, and why customers will keep paying it. If not, the application is not ready for credit. Credit must decline where the business model:\\n- depends on a single contract or customer > 50% of revenue without contractual protection beyond the loan tenor, unless approved by Senior Credit Manager;\\n- has no evidenced path to positive operating cash flow within the facility tenor (outside Start-up policy);\\n- relies on regulatory permissions the business does not hold.',\n",
       "  \"9. Financial Analysis\\nRatios are evidence, not decisions: every ratio outside its warning threshold must be explained in the credit application, but no single ratio alone approves or declines a facility. All ratios must be calculated by the bank's spreading tool from spread financial statements, using Normalised EBITDA (Section 10.4) unless stated otherwise. IFRS 16 lease liabilities are included in debt, and lease costs excluded from EBITDA, consistently on both sides.\\nSpreading standard. Financial statements must be spread into the bank's standard chart (Appendix B) within 10 working days of receipt. Spreading must be done or checked by a person other than the RM for TCE > £1m. Warning thresholds below are Northbridge defaults; Sector Guides (Section 30) may tighten them.\",\n",
       "  '9.1 Profitability\\n| - |\\n| Revenue growth, Formula = (Revenueₜ - Revenueₜ₋₁) / Revenueₜ₋₁. Revenue growth, What it tells the lender = Demand and market position. Revenue growth, Typical interpretation = Stable/positive growth at or above inflation. Revenue growth, Warning threshold = Decline > 10% p.a., or growth > 30% p.a. (overtrading). Revenue growth, Limitations and industry notes = Distorted by acquisitions, disposals, price inflation, FX. Strip inflation to see volume. Gross margin, Formula = Gross profit / Revenue. Gross margin, What it tells the lender = Pricing power and direct cost control. Gross margin, Typical interpretation = Stable or improving. Gross margin, Warning threshold = Fall > 3pp year on year. Gross margin, Limitations and industry notes = Classification of costs between COGS and overheads varies; compare only like-for-like. EBITDA margin, Formula = Normalised EBITDA / Revenue. EBITDA margin, What it tells the lender = Operating cash- generation capacity. EBITDA margin, Typical interpretation = Sector- dependent: wholesale 3-6%, manufacturing 8-15%, services 10-20%. EBITDA margin, Warning threshold = Fall > 2pp YoY, or below sector Guide floor. EBITDA margin, Limitations and industry notes = Ignores capex intensity; a high- EBITDA business can still be cash- poor. Operating margin, Formula = EBIT / Revenue. Operating margin, What it tells the lender = Profit after asset consumption. Operating margin, Typical interpretation = Positive and stable. Operating margin, Warning threshold = < 3%. Operating margin, Limitations and industry notes = Depreciation policy differences. Net profit margin, Formula = Profit after tax / Revenue. Net profit margin, What it tells the lender = Bottom-line profitability. Net profit margin, Typical interpretation = Positive. Net profit',\n",
       "  '9.1 Profitability\\n| - |\\n margin, Warning threshold = Loss in any of last 2 years. Net profit margin, Limitations and industry notes = Affected by tax, interest and one- offs. ROA, Formula = EBIT / Average total assets. ROA, What it tells the lender = Efficiency of the asset base. ROA, Typical interpretation = > cost of debt. ROA, Warning threshold = < cost of debt. ROA, Limitations and industry notes = Old, depreciated assets flatter ROA. ROE, Formula = Profit after tax / Average equity. ROE, What it tells the lender = Return to owners. ROE, Typical interpretation = 10-20%. ROE, Warning threshold = Negative; or > 40% with low equity (thin capital). ROE, Limitations and industry notes = Meaningless with negative or very low equity |\\n',\n",
       "  '9.2 Liquidity\\n| - |\\n| Current ratio, Formula = Current assets / Current liabilities. Current ratio, What it tells the lender = Short-term solvency. Current ratio, Typical interpretation = 1.2x-2.0x. Current ratio, Warning threshold = < 1.0x. Current ratio, Limitations and industry notes = Inflated by slow stock or bad debts; retailers and food businesses with fast cash cycles operate safely < 1.0x. Quick ratio, Formula = (Current assets - Inventory) / Current liabilities. Quick ratio, What it tells the lender = Liquidity without selling stock. Quick ratio, Typical interpretation = 0.8x-1.2x. Quick ratio, Warning threshold = < 0.7x. Quick ratio, Limitations and industry notes = Receivables quality matters; check ageing. Cash ratio, Formula = Cash / Current liabilities. Cash ratio, What it tells the lender = Immediate liquidity. Cash ratio, Typical interpretation = 0.1x-0.3x. Cash ratio, Warning threshold = < 0.05x with overdraft > 80% utilised. Cash ratio, Limitations and industry notes = Point-in-time; year-end window dressing. Working capital, Formula = Current assets - Current liabilities. Working capital, What it tells the lender = Buffer funding the operating cycle. Working capital, Typical interpretation = Positive, growing with sales. Working capital, Warning threshold = Negative (outside sectors with structural negative WC). Working capital, Limitations and industry notes = Absolute number; compare to revenue. Operating cash conversion, Formula = Cash from operations before tax / Normalised EBITDA. Operating cash conversion, What it tells the lender = How much EBITDA turns into cash. Operating cash conversion, Typical interpretation = 80%-100%. Operating cash conversion, Warning threshold = < 70% for 2 consecutive years. Operating cash conversion,',\n",
       "  '9.2 Liquidity\\n| - |\\n Limitations and industry notes = One-year swings from working capital; use 3- year average |\\n',\n",
       "  \"9.3 Leverage\\n| - |\\n| Total debt / EBITDA, Formula = (All interest- bearing debt + leases) / Normalised EBITDA. Total debt / EBITDA, What it tells the lender = Years of EBITDA to repay debt. Total debt / EBITDA, Typical interpretation = ≤ 2.5x comfortable. Total debt / EBITDA, Warning threshold = > 3.0x; policy max for cash- flow lending 3.0x (hospitality 2.5x), 3.5x with Central Credit Committee. Total debt / EBITDA, Limitations and industry notes = Volatile EBITDA makes this unstable; use LTM and forecast. Net debt / EBITDA, Formula = (Total debt - unrestricted cash) / Normalised EBITDA. Net debt / EBITDA, What it tells the lender = Leverage net of liquidity. Net debt / EBITDA, Typical interpretation = ≤ 2.0x comfortable. Net debt / EBITDA, Warning threshold = > 3.0x. Net debt / EBITDA, Limitations and industry notes = Cash must be genuinely free (not trapped, not customer deposits or VAT). Debt / equity (gearing), Formula = Total debt / Tangible net worth. Debt / equity (gearing), What it tells the lender = Owners' capital at risk vs lender's. Debt / equity (gearing), Typical interpretation = < 1.0x. Debt / equity (gearing), Warning threshold = > 2.0x, or negative TNW. Debt / equity (gearing), Limitations and industry notes = Director loans: treat as equity only if formally subordinated. Debt service coverage (DSCR), Formula = CFADS / (Scheduled principal + interest + lease payments). Debt service coverage (DSCR), What it tells the lender = Ability to meet all debt payments\",\n",
       "  '9.3 Leverage\\n| - |\\n from cash flow. Debt service coverage (DSCR), Typical interpretation = 1.5x+ strong. Debt service coverage (DSCR), Warning threshold = < 1.25x policy minimum (hospitality 1.35x); < 1.10x decline outside policy. Debt service coverage (DSCR), Limitations and industry notes = Depends on how CFADS is defined (Section 10.2); forecast DSCR must use the bank case |\\n',\n",
       "  '9.4 Coverage\\nInterest cover, Formula = EBIT / Net finance costs. Interest cover, What it tells the lender = Profit buffer over interest. Interest cover, Typical interpretation = > 4.0x. Interest cover, Warning threshold = < 3.0x policy standard; < 2.0x decline outside policy. Interest cover, Limitations and industry notes = Ignores principal; flattering for interest-only debt. EBITDA interest cover, Formula = Normalised EBITDA / Net finance costs. EBITDA interest cover, What it tells the lender = Cash-proxy cover of interest. EBITDA interest cover, Typical interpretation = > 6.0x. EBITDA interest cover, Warning threshold = < 4.0x. EBITDA interest cover, Limitations and industry notes = Ignores capex and tax. Fixed-charge cover (FCCR), Formula = (EBITDA - Rent - Maintenance capex - Tax) / (Interest + Rent + Scheduled principal). Fixed-charge cover (FCCR), What it tells the lender = Coverage of all fixed obligations. Fixed-charge cover (FCCR), Typical interpretation = > 1.2x. Fixed-charge cover (FCCR), Warning threshold = < 1.1x. Fixed-charge cover (FCCR), Limitations and industry notes = Essential for retail and hospitality where rent is the largest fixed cost. DSCR, Formula = See 9.3. DSCR, What it tells the lender = Primary repayment test. DSCR, Typical interpretation = See 9.3. DSCR, Warning threshold = See 9.3. DSCR, Limitations and industry notes = See 9.3',\n",
       "  '9.5 Efficiency\\n| - |\\n| Debtor days, Formula = Trade receivables / Revenue × 365. Debtor days, What it tells the lender = Speed of customer collection. Debtor days, Typical interpretation = 30-60 days B2B; ~0 retail. Debtor days, Warning threshold = Increase > 15 days YoY, or > 90 days. Debtor days, Limitations and industry notes = Year-end timing; VAT-inclusive receivables vs VAT-exclusive revenue overstate days by up to 20%. Creditor days, Formula = Trade payables / Cost of sales × 365. Creditor days, What it tells the lender = Speed of paying suppliers. Creditor days, Typical interpretation = 30-60 days. Creditor days, Warning threshold = > 90 days, or rising sharply (stretching suppliers). Creditor days, Limitations and industry notes = Using cost of sales understates days where overheads are on trade credit. Inventory days, Formula = Inventory / Cost of sales × 365. Inventory days, What it tells the lender = Stock holding. Inventory days, Typical interpretation = Sector- dependent: chilled food 5- 20, manufacturing 40-90. Inventory days, Warning threshold = Increase > 20 days YoY. Inventory days, Limitations and industry notes = Obsolete stock inflates value; check provisions. Cash conversion cycle, Formula = Debtor days + Inventory days - Creditor days. Cash conversion cycle, What it tells the lender = Days of working capital to fund. Cash conversion cycle, Typical interpretation = Stable. Cash conversion cycle, Warning threshold = Increase > 20 days YoY. Cash conversion cycle, Limitations and industry notes = Composite inherits all component limitations. Asset turnover, Formula = Revenue / Average total assets. Asset turnover, What it tells the lender = Revenue generated per £ of assets. Asset turnover, Typical interpretation = Sector- dependent. Asset turnover, Warning threshold =',\n",
       "  '9.5 Efficiency\\n| - |\\n Fall > 20% YoY. Asset turnover, Limitations and industry notes = Capital-intensive sectors structurally low |\\n',\n",
       "  '9.6 Cash flow\\n| - |\\n| Operating cash flow (CFO), Formula = Per cash-flow statement, or EBITDA ± Δ working capital - tax. Operating cash flow (CFO), What it tells the lender = Cash generated from trading. Operating cash flow (CFO), Typical interpretation = Positive and ≥ 70% of EBITDA. Operating cash flow (CFO), Warning threshold = Negative in any year. Operating cash flow (CFO), Limitations and industry notes = Small-company accounts often lack a cash-flow statement; derive from balance-sheet movements. Free cash flow (FCF), Formula = CFO - Capex. Free cash flow (FCF), What it tells the lender = Cash available after investment. Free cash flow (FCF), Typical interpretation = Positive. Free cash flow (FCF), Warning threshold = Negative for 2 consecutive years (excluding funded growth capex). Free cash flow (FCF), Limitations and industry notes = Lumpy capex distorts single years. CFO / debt, Formula = CFO / Total debt. CFO / debt, What it tells the lender = Cash-based leverage. CFO / debt, Typical interpretation = > 25%. CFO / debt, Warning threshold = < 15%. CFO / debt, Limitations and industry notes = Inverse of a payback period. CFO / interest, Formula = CFO / Interest paid. CFO / interest, What it tells the lender = Cash-based interest cover. CFO / interest, Typical interpretation = > 5.0x. CFO / interest, Warning threshold = < 3.0x. CFO / interest, Limitations and industry notes = -. Cash conversion, Formula = FCF / Normalised EBITDA. Cash conversion, What it tells the lender = Quality of earnings. Cash conversion, Typical interpretation = >',\n",
       "  '9.6 Cash flow\\n| - |\\n 50%. Cash conversion, Warning threshold = < 30%. Cash conversion, Limitations and industry notes = Capex-heavy sectors naturally lower |\\n',\n",
       "  \"9.7 Trend and peer analysis\\nEvery financial analysis must show at least the last 3 periods side by side (2 where only 2 exist), the year-to-date position, and the sector median from the bank's peer database where available. The narrative must explain the direction of travel for each warning-threshold breach, not only the latest value.\",\n",
       "  \"10. Cash-Flow Assessment\\nSustainable operating cash flow is the primary repayment source for all SME lending except investment and development property. The core test is a DSCR of at least 1.25x on Normalised CFADS (1.35x for hospitality), demonstrated on historical performance and, where new debt funds growth, on the bank's own forecast case.\",\n",
       "  '10.1 What \"sustainable\" means\\nCash generation is sustainable when it is (a) recurring, (b) achievable without further borrowing, (c) after the owners have taken what they realistically need to live on, and (d) after the capex needed to keep the business running at its current level. Cash flow that depends on one-off income, deferred tax, stretched suppliers or skipped maintenance is not sustainable.',\n",
       "  \"10.2 CFADS methodology\\n\\\\text{CFADS} = \\\\text{Normalised EBITDA} - \\\\text{Cash tax} - \\\\text{Maintenance capex} - \\\\Delta\\\\text{Working capital} - \\\\text{Sustainable owner drawings}\\n\\\\text{DSCR} = \\\\frac{\\\\text{CFADS}}{\\\\text{Scheduled principal} + \\\\text{Interest} + \\\\text{Lease and HP payments}}\\nNormalised EBITDA, Policy treatment = Section 10.4. Use the lower of last full-year and LTM where management accounts show deterioration. Cash tax, Policy treatment = Tax actually payable on the normalised profit, not the P&L charge. For sole traders and partnerships, deduct the principals' income tax and NIC on business profits. Maintenance capex, Policy treatment = The higher of (i) the 3-year average capex excluding identified growth capex and (ii) 50% of annual depreciation. Growth capex funded by new debt is excluded. Δ Working capital, Policy treatment = The investment needed to support forecast revenue at current working-capital days. Releases of working capital from falling sales are not counted as CFADS. Sustainable owner drawings, Policy treatment = For companies: dividends required to cover directors' personal living costs where salary has been normalised to below that level, minimum £30k per working owner (illustrative). For sole traders/partners: actual drawings, minimum £30k per working principal. Debt service, Policy treatment = All scheduled payments to all lenders over the next 12 months, including HP and lease rentals, the proposed facility on its contractual profile, and RCF interest assuming 50% average utilisation (100% if historically > 75% utilised). Interest rate, Policy treatment = Contractual rate for fixed debt. Floating debt at the current reference rate plus margin, plus the policy stress in Section 14\",\n",
       "  \"10.3 Historical vs forecast cash flow\\nRefinancing or general term lending, Primary test = Historical pro-forma DSCR (latest year's CFADS against proposed total debt service). Refinancing or general term lending, Minimum = 1.25x. Growth or capex lending where new cash flow is needed to service new debt, Primary test = Historical pro-forma DSCR and bank-case forecast DSCR by the end of year 2. Growth or capex lending where new cash flow is needed to service new debt, Minimum = Historical ≥ 1.10x; bank case ≥ 1.25x. Acquisition finance, Primary test = Combined historical pro-forma DSCR excluding unproven synergies. Acquisition finance, Minimum = 1.30x. Start-up, Primary test = Section 31 liquidity runway; forecast DSCR. Start-up, Minimum = Bank case ≥ 1.20x by end of year 2\\nThe bank case. Credit must build its own bank case and must not approve on management forecasts alone. Unless revenue is contracted, the bank case must haircut forecast incremental EBITDA by at least 40% and assume no improvement in margin over the latest 12 months. Contracted revenue (signed contract, ≥ the facility tenor or 3 years if shorter, with a creditworthy counterparty) may be taken at 90%.\",\n",
       "  '10.4 Normalisation of EBITDA\\nNormalisation must be evidence-based, symmetrical (reductions as well as add-backs) and capped. Total add-backs must not exceed 15% of reported EBITDA without Senior Credit Manager approval, and 25% in any case.',\n",
       "  \"10.4 Normalisation of EBITDA\\n| - |\\n| One-off costs (e.g. redundancy, site closure), Add back? = Yes, if genuinely non-recurring. One-off costs (e.g. redundancy, site closure), Treatment and evidence = Invoices or board minutes; not if the business has incurred similar costs in 2 of the last 3 years. Exceptional income (e.g. insurance receipts, asset sale profits, grants), Add back? = Deduct. Exceptional income (e.g. insurance receipts, asset sale profits, grants), Treatment and evidence = Remove fully from EBITDA. Owner remuneration below market, Add back? = Deduct the shortfall. Owner remuneration below market, Treatment and evidence = Normalise to a market salary for the role (bank's benchmark table), e.g. MD of a £10m-£25m business £100k-£130k (illustrative). Owner remuneration above market, Add back? = Add back the excess, max 50% of excess. Owner remuneration above market, Treatment and evidence = Only if a binding reduction is documented (board minute plus covenant). Related-party expenses, Add back? = Adjust to arm's length. Related-party expenses, Treatment and evidence = Management charges, consultancy to family members, intercompany recharges. Personal expenses through the business, Add back? = Add back if evidenced and ceased. Personal expenses through the business, Treatment and evidence = Cars, travel, school fees; require written confirmation they have stopped. Rent paid to related parties (e.g. directors' SIPP), Add back? = Adjust to market rent. Rent paid to related parties (e.g. directors' SIPP), Treatment and evidence = If below market, deduct the shortfall. If above\",\n",
       "  '10.4 Normalisation of EBITDA\\n| - |\\n market, add back excess only if the lease is amended. Director loans, Add back? = No EBITDA effect. Director loans, Treatment and evidence = Repayments of director loans count as debt service unless subordinated by deed of postponement |\\n',\n",
       "  '10.4 Normalisation of EBITDA\\nNon-recurring legal costs, Add back? = Add back max 50%. Non-recurring legal costs, Treatment and evidence = Litigation can recur; full add-back only if the matter is closed and evidenced. Restructuring costs, Add back? = Add back if programme complete. Restructuring costs, Treatment and evidence = Not while the programme is ongoing or repeated. Acquisition costs (deal fees), Add back? = Yes. Acquisition costs (deal fees), Treatment and evidence = Transaction-specific invoices only; integration costs only for 12 months post-completion. COVID-era effects (grants, business rates relief, furlough, CBILS/BBLS interest relief), Add back? = Deduct income; costs not added back. COVID-era effects (grants, business rates relief, furlough, CBILS/BBLS interest relief), Treatment and evidence = Periods to FY2022 are not representative; place more weight on post-2022 performance. Accounting changes (IFRS 16, revenue recognition, capitalised development costs), Add back? = Restate to a consistent basis. Accounting changes (IFRS 16, revenue recognition, capitalised development costs), Treatment and evidence = Capitalised development costs are deducted from EBITDA as if expensed, unless supported by a funded R&D tax credit receivable. Unrealised FX and fair-value movements, Add back? = Remove. Unrealised FX and fair-value movements, Treatment and evidence = Non-cash. Pro-forma synergies, Add back? = No. Pro-forma synergies, Treatment and evidence = Excluded from the credit case; may be shown as upside only',\n",
       "  '10.5 Seasonality and volatility\\n- Seasonal businesses (agriculture, tourism, retail, construction) must provide a monthly cash-flow forecast. Peak debt must be covered by committed facilities with ≥ 15% headroom at the forecast peak.\\n- Where EBITDA has varied by more than 25% between any two of the last 3 years, CFADS must use the 3-year average or the latest year, whichever is lower.\\n- Businesses with high operating leverage (fixed costs > 60% of total costs) must be tested with the moderate stress applied at month level, not only annually.',\n",
       "  '13. Qualitative Credit Assessment\\nQualitative assessment captures risks the numbers do not yet show. It is scored on defined scales so that two credit officers reach the same view, and it can move the model grade by at most one notch without an override.',\n",
       "  '13.1 Northbridge \"5 Cs\" adapted for modern SME lending\\nThe traditional 5 Cs remain useful, but Northbridge separates them by what they actually inform: PD, LGD or structure.\\nCharacter, Traditional meaning = Honesty and willingness to repay. Character, Northbridge interpretation = Management integrity, transparency, governance, reporting quality and track record with lenders, HMRC and suppliers. Character, Informs = PD (management score); KYC. Capacity, Traditional meaning = Ability to repay. Capacity, Northbridge interpretation = Normalised CFADS, DSCR under base and stress, bank-case forecasts. Capacity, Informs = PD (cash-flow score); approval. Capital, Traditional meaning = Owners\\' stake. Capital, Northbridge interpretation = Tangible net worth, owner equity in the deal, willingness to inject capital in stress. Capital, Informs = PD; structure. Collateral, Traditional meaning = Security. Collateral, Northbridge interpretation = Realisable value after haircuts, ranking and enforceability. Collateral, Informs = LGD only. Conditions, Traditional meaning = Economic environment. Conditions, Northbridge interpretation = Sector outlook, macro sensitivities, climate and transition risk, regulatory change, plus facility conditions (covenants, CPs). Conditions, Informs = PD (industry/external); structure',\n",
       "  '13.2 Management\\nExperience, Strong (score 3) = > 10 years in sector; has managed through a downturn. Experience, Adequate (2) = 3-10 years. Experience, Weak (1) = < 3 years, or new to sector. Succession, Strong (score 3) = Documented plan; second- tier management capable. Succession, Adequate (2) = Informal plan. Succession, Weak (1) = No succession; owner > 65 with no plan. Key-person dependency, Strong (score 3) = No single person critical; key-person insurance held. Key-person dependency, Adequate (2) = One key person, insured. Key-person dependency, Weak (1) = One key person, uninsured. Governance, Strong (score 3) = Board with non-executive or adviser; regular board minutes. Governance, Adequate (2) = Directors meet informally but record decisions. Governance, Weak (1) = No governance; decisions undocumented\\nFinancial sophistication, Strong (score 3) = Monthly management accounts within 20 days; budgets met ±10%. Financial sophistication, Adequate (2) = Quarterly MI; budgets met ±20%. Financial sophistication, Weak (1) = No MI; accounts filed late. Track record with lenders, Strong (score 3) = No arrears or covenant breaches. Track record with lenders, Adequate (2) = Minor, resolved. Track record with lenders, Weak (1) = Recent breaches, arrears or defaults',\n",
       "  \"13.3 Business model\\nCompetitive advantage, Strong = Clear, durable (brand, licence, proprietary process, location). Competitive advantage, Adequate = Some differentiation. Competitive advantage, Weak = Price-taker in a commodity market. Customer concentration, Strong = Largest customer < 15%; top 5 < 40%. Customer concentration, Adequate = Largest 15%-30%. Customer concentration, Weak = Largest > 30% (policy trigger) or top 5 > 70%. Supplier concentration, Strong = Multiple suppliers per input. Supplier concentration, Adequate = Some dependency with alternatives. Supplier concentration, Weak = Single-source critical input. Pricing power, Strong = Passes cost increases through within 3 months. Pricing power, Adequate = Partial pass-through with lag. Pricing power, Weak = Fixed-price contracts > 12 months without indexation. Recurring revenue, Strong = > 60% contracted or subscription. Recurring revenue, Adequate = 30%-60%. Recurring revenue, Weak = < 30%. Contract quality, Strong = Long-term, creditworthy counterparties, termination protection. Contract quality, Adequate = Annual contracts. Contract quality, Weak = Purchase-order basis; terminable at will\\nCustomer concentration rule. Where one customer exceeds 30% of revenue, the application must assess that customer's creditworthiness, the contract terms and remaining tenor, and the cash-flow effect of losing that customer. Above 50%, Senior Credit Manager approval is required (Section 6.5).\",\n",
       "  '13.4 Industry\\nThe Northbridge sector risk score (1 = low to 5 = high) is set by Portfolio Credit Risk for each SIC code from: cyclicality (correlation of sector default rate with GDP), regulatory intensity, technological disruption, commodity exposure, and economic sensitivity to interest rates and consumer demand. The RM may not change the sector score, but must explain where the obligor differs from its sector (for example, a contractor with only public-sector clients).',\n",
       "  \"13.5 External environment\\nEach application must state the obligor's sensitivity to the following, quantified where material (> 10% EBITDA impact):\\nInterest rates, What to assess = Floating-rate share of debt; impact of +300bps on DSCR (Section 14). Inflation, What to assess = Cost-base composition; ability to reprice contracts. Consumer demand, What to assess = B2C exposure; discretionary vs essential spend. Energy prices, What to assess = Energy as % of costs; contract fix expiry dates. Labour costs, What to assess = Staff costs as % of revenue; National Living Wage exposure; skills shortages\\nExchange rates, What to assess = Unhedged FX purchases or sales > 10% of revenue; hedging policy\",\n",
       "  '14. Stress Testing\\nEvery application with TCE above £250k, and every Restricted-sector or grade 7-10 application at any size, must be stress tested on the bank case. The borrower must pass the mild stress outright, survive the moderate stress on cash flow or committed liquidity, and show adequate liquidity and security cover under the severe stress.',\n",
       "  '14.1 Standard scenarios\\nShocks apply together, to the bank-case year in which debt service is highest (usually year 1 or 2), and are held for 12 months.\\nMild, Revenue = -10%. Mild, EBITDA margin = -2 percentage points. Mild, Interest rate (floating debt) = +100bps. Mild, Working capital = No release from lower sales. Moderate, Revenue = -20%. Moderate, EBITDA margin = -4 percentage points. Moderate, Interest rate (floating debt) = +300bps. Moderate, Working capital = No release; debtor days +10. Severe, Revenue = -30%. Severe, EBITDA margin = -6 percentage points. Severe, Interest rate (floating debt) = +500bps. Severe, Working capital = No release; debtor days +15; supplier credit -10 days\\nMitigating management actions (cutting dividends, deferring maintenance capex) may be shown separately but must not be used to pass the mild or moderate test, except that discretionary distributions may be removed in the severe scenario.',\n",
       "  \"14.2 Pass criteria\\nMild, Test = Stressed DSCR. Mild, Requirement = ≥ 1.10x. Mild, If failed = Decline or restructure (reduce amount, extend tenor within asset life, add equity). Moderate, Test = Stressed DSCR, or liquidity cover of the shortfall. Moderate, Requirement = DSCR ≥ 1.00x, or cash plus undrawn committed facilities covers ≥ 12 months of the CFADS shortfall. Moderate, If failed = Decline or restructure; approval outside this requires a Policy Exception at Senior Credit Manager level. Severe, Test = Liquidity runway and forced-sale security cover. Severe, Requirement = Runway ≥ 6 months and forced-sale security cover ≥ 80% of Northbridge exposure (≥ 100% for grades 7-8). Severe, If failed = Reduce unsecured exposure, add security or guarantees; otherwise Senior Credit Manager approval with documented rationale\\nThe severe scenario is not a survival test. Many sound SMEs would not service full debt through a 30% revenue fall; its purpose is to size the bank's potential loss and the time available to act.\",\n",
       "  '14.3 Stress metrics\\nDSCR stress, Formula / method = Stressed CFADS / Stressed debt service. Interest coverage stress, Formula / method = Stressed EBIT / Stressed interest; must remain ≥ 1.5x under mild. Liquidity runway (months), Formula / method = (Cash + undrawn committed facilities) / Monthly CFADS shortfall. Covenant headroom, Formula / method = Base-case headroom must be ≥ 20% for leverage and DSCR covenants at the first test date (15% with Credit Manager approval). The mild stress must not breach any financial covenant in the first 12 months. Cash burn, Formula / method = For loss-making or start-up businesses: monthly operating cash outflow under base and moderate stress. Refinancing risk, Formula / method = Any balloon > 30% must be refinanceable at maturity at +200bps on the then-reference rate, within policy LTV and DSCR, using the bank case for the maturity year\\n\\\\text{Runway (months)} = \\\\frac{\\\\text{Cash} + \\\\text{Undrawn committed facilities}}{(\\\\text{Stressed debt service} - \\\\text{Stressed CFADS}) / 12}',\n",
       "  '14.4 Sector-specific stresses\\nThese replace the generic margin shock for the named sector where they are more severe.\\nHospitality, Additional or replacement stress = Revenue -25% (moderate); energy costs +50%; wages +8%. Construction, Additional or replacement stress = Largest contract cancelled or delayed 6 months; retentions unpaid; one key subcontractor fails. Food manufacturing and processing, Additional or replacement stress = Loss of largest customer (pro-rata over 6 months); input commodity +20% with 6-month pass-through lag. Agriculture, Additional or replacement stress = Yield -20%; output price -15%; support-payment transition per the latest scheme schedule. Transport and logistics, Additional or replacement stress = Fuel +30%; driver wages +10%. Retail, Additional or replacement stress = Like-for-like sales -15%; rent review +10%. Recruitment, Additional or replacement stress = Largest client lost; debtor days +20. Commercial property (investment), Additional or replacement stress = Rental income -20% via voids; capital value -25%; interest +300bps (ICR test, Section 32). Import / export, Additional or replacement stress = GBP ±15% against main trading currency, whichever direction hurts. Energy-intensive manufacturing, Additional or replacement stress = Energy +50%; carbon cost per Climate Risk Standard transition scenario',\n",
       "  '14.5 Climate overlay\\nFor property in high flood-risk zones and obligors in carbon-intensive sectors, the severe scenario must also include the relevant physical or transition shock from Section 37 (for example, uninsurable flood risk reducing property value by 30%).',\n",
       "  '15. Lending Limits\\nThe maximum Northbridge will lend to an obligor is the lowest of four independent limits: the cash-flow limit, the product limit, the grade-adjusted unsecured limit, and the concentration limit. Security can increase what is safe to lend only within the cash-flow limit; it never lifts it.',\n",
       "  '15.1 Cash-flow (debt capacity) limit\\nTotal senior debt to all lenders must not exceed:\\nSenior debt / Normalised EBITDA, Standard = 3.0x. Senior debt / Normalised EBITDA, Hospitality / Restricted sectors = 2.5x. Senior debt / Normalised EBITDA, With Central Credit Committee approval = 3.5x (never above 4.0x under this policy). Debt service at policy DSCR, Standard = Debt service ≤ CFADS / 1.25. Debt service at policy DSCR, Hospitality / Restricted sectors = ≤ CFADS / 1.35. Debt service at policy DSCR, With Central Credit Committee approval = ≤ CFADS / 1.15\\n\\\\text{Max annual debt service} = \\\\frac{\\\\text{CFADS}}{\\\\text{Minimum DSCR}}, \\\\qquad \\\\text{Max new debt} = \\\\text{PV of available debt service over tenor at the stressed rate}',\n",
       "  '15.2 Product limits\\nOverdraft, Maximum = Lower of 15% of annual turnover and £2m (20% / £3m for seasonal businesses with a monthly forecast). Unsecured term loan, Maximum = Lower of 10% of turnover and the grade-adjusted unsecured limit (15.3). Business credit card, Maximum = £25k per card, £100k per obligor. Invoice discounting, Maximum = 85% of eligible debts. Owner-occupied property loan, Maximum = 70% LTV and DSCR from trading cash flow. Investment property loan, Maximum = 65% LTV and ICR ≥ 1.75x at stressed rate. Development, Maximum = 60% LTGDV, 75% LTC. Single SME obligor (TCE), Maximum = £50m (above this, Corporate Credit Policy)',\n",
       "  '15.3 Grade-adjusted unsecured exposure\\nUnsecured exposure means exposure not covered by forced-sale value of tangible security (personal guarantees do not count as security for this limit).\\nMicro, Grades 1-4 = £100k. Micro, Grades 5-6 = £50k. Micro, Grade 7 = £25k. Micro, Grade 8+ = Nil. Small, Grades 1-4 = £500k. Small, Grades 5-6 = £250k. Small, Grade 7 = £100k. Small, Grade 8+ = Nil. Medium, Grades 1-4 = £2m. Medium, Grades 5-6 = £1m. Medium, Grade 7 = £500k. Medium, Grade 8+ = Nil. Upper Mid-Market, Grades 1-4 = £5m. Upper Mid-Market, Grades 5-6 = £2.5m. Upper Mid-Market, Grade 7 = £1m. Upper Mid-Market, Grade 8+ = Nil\\nUnsecured exposure must also not exceed 1.0x Normalised EBITDA for grades 1-6, and 0.5x for grade 7.',\n",
       "  \"15.4 Concentration limits\\nSingle connected group (TCE), Maximum = £50m, and ≤ 2% of SME book Tier 1 capital allocation. Single connected group (TCE), Owner = Central Credit Committee. Connected-party exposure to directors' other businesses, Maximum = Aggregated into the group TCE (Section 33). Connected-party exposure to directors' other businesses, Owner = Credit. Any single sector (excl. owner- occupied property), Maximum = 18% of SME exposure (Amber), 22% (Red). Any single sector (excl. owner- occupied property), Owner = GCRC. Commercial real estate investment + development, Maximum = 25% of SME exposure; development ≤ 4%. Commercial real estate investment + development, Owner = GCRC. Hospitality, Maximum = 6% of SME exposure. Hospitality, Owner = GCRC. Construction, Maximum = 5% of SME exposure. Construction, Owner = GCRC. Grade 8-10 exposure, Maximum = 7% of SME exposure. Grade 8-10 exposure, Owner = GCRC. Any single UK region (ITL1), Maximum = 25% of SME exposure. Any single UK region (ITL1), Owner = GCRC\\nApplications that would cause a portfolio concentration limit to breach Amber require Central Credit Committee approval and notification to Portfolio Credit Risk; at Red, approval requires GCRC.\",\n",
       "  '15.5 Worked limit test (illustrative)\\nA grade-5 Small-segment engineering firm, turnover £6m, Normalised EBITDA £720k, CFADS £480k, existing debt service £150k, no property.\\nCash-flow (leverage), Calculation = 3.0 × £720k. Cash-flow (leverage), Limit = £2.16m senior debt. Cash-flow (DSCR), Calculation = £480k / 1.25 - £150k = £234k available annual debt service → 5- year amortising at 8% stressed. Cash-flow (DSCR), Limit = c.£935k new debt. Unsecured (grade 5, Small), Calculation = Lower of £250k and 1.0x EBITDA. Unsecured (grade 5, Small), Limit = £250k unsecured. Product (unsecured term loan), Calculation = 10% of £6m. Product (unsecured term loan), Limit = £600k\\nMaximum new lending is c.£935k, of which no more than £250k may be unsecured. The remaining c. £685k requires tangible security with forced-sale value at least equal to that amount.',\n",
       "  \"30. Sector-Specific Underwriting\\nSector Guides tighten the general policy where a sector's risks are specific; they never loosen it. The summaries below are the minimum considerations; the full Sector Guides add benchmark ratios from the bank's peer database.\",\n",
       "  '30. Sector-Specific Underwriting\\n| - |\\n| Retail, Main risks = Online shift; rent and rates; discretionary demand; stock obsolescence. Retail, Key ratios = Like-for-like sales; gross margin; FCCR (rent- adjusted); stock turn. Retail, Key documents = Lease schedule; store-level P&L; stock ageing. Retail, Stress scenario = LFL -15%; rent review +10%. Retail, Security considerations = Leasehold has little value; stock 50%-80% haircut. Retail, Warning indicators = Store closures; rising markdowns; supplier credit insurance withdrawn. Hospitality, Main risks = Energy, labour, consumer demand; licensing; high failure rate. Hospitality, Key ratios = EBITDA margin; FCCR; wet/dry/rooms mix; RevPAR (hotels). Hospitality, Key documents = Licence; trading by outlet; energy contracts. Hospitality, Stress scenario = Revenue -25%; energy +50%; wages +8%. Hospitality, Security considerations = Trading property valued on trading potential, 40% haircut. Hospitality, Warning indicators = Falling covers; HMRC TTP; energy contract expiring unhedged. Construction, Main risks = Fixed-price contracts; retentions; payment- chain insolvency; cost inflation. Construction, Key ratios = Order book cover; WIP and retentions vs equity; gross margin per contract. Construction, Key documents = Contract list with margins; WIP schedule; bonding lines. Construction, Stress scenario = Largest contract cancelled or delayed 6 months. Construction, Security considerations = Receivables often disputed; WIP worth little. Construction, Warning indicators = Margin erosion on live contracts; claims; subcontractor disputes. Manufacturing, Main risks = Input costs; customer concentration; capex intensity; energy. Manufacturing, Key ratios = Gross margin; capacity utilisation; capex / depreciation.',\n",
       "  '30. Sector-Specific Underwriting\\n| - |\\n Manufacturing, Key documents = Customer contracts; order book; asset register. Manufacturing, Stress scenario = Loss of top customer; input +20%. Manufacturing, Security considerations = P&M: general vs specialist haircuts. Manufacturing, Warning indicators = Utilisation < 65%; delayed capex; quality failures. Professional services, Main risks = Key-person; partner exits; PI claims; WIP recovery. Professional services, Key ratios = Fee per fee- earner; lock- up days (WIP + debtors); partner capital. Professional services, Key documents = Partnership deed; PI insurance; regulator status. Professional services, Stress scenario = Loss of top 2 fee- earners; lock-up +20 days. Professional services, Security considerations = Few tangible assets; partner capital lock-in. Professional services, Warning indicators = Partner departures; regulatory investigations; rising lock-up. Healthcare, Main risks = Regulatory rating; staffing; public-funding rates; occupancy (care). Healthcare, Key ratios = Occupancy; staff cost % of revenue; EBITDARM. Healthcare, Key documents = CQC / regulator reports; contracts with commissioners. Healthcare, Stress scenario = Occupancy -8pp; agency staff costs +25%. Healthcare, Security considerations = Care homes: specialist trading property. Healthcare, Warning indicators = Regulator rating downgrade; rising agency use. Transport and logistics, Main risks = Fuel, drivers, utilisation; contract renewal. Transport and logistics, Key ratios = Fleet utilisation; revenue per vehicle; FCCR. Transport and logistics, Key documents = Fleet schedule; operator licence; customer contracts. Transport and logistics, Stress scenario = Fuel +30%; driver wages +10%. Transport and logistics, Security considerations = Vehicles: 40% haircut; residual risk. Transport and logistics, Warning indicators = Operator licence issues;',\n",
       "  '30. Sector-Specific Underwriting\\n| - |\\n ageing fleet; lost contracts |\\n',\n",
       "  '30. Sector-Specific Underwriting\\n| - |\\n| Agriculture, Main risks = Weather, commodity prices, subsidy transition, disease. Agriculture, Key ratios = Output per hectare; cost per unit; gearing vs land value. Agriculture, Key documents = Farm business plan; support- scheme correspondence; land titles. Agriculture, Stress scenario = Yield -20%; price -15%. Agriculture, Security considerations = Land: low LTV, illiquid; livestock perishable. Agriculture, Warning indicators = Disease outbreaks; scheme payment delays; rising rent. Wholesale, Main risks = Thin margins; working capital; customer credit risk. Wholesale, Key ratios = Gross margin; debtor/stock days; CCC. Wholesale, Key documents = Aged debtors and stock; credit insurance. Wholesale, Stress scenario = Margin -1pp; debtor days +15. Wholesale, Security considerations = Receivables and stock; invoice finance preferred. Wholesale, Warning indicators = Credit insurance limits cut; stock build-up. Technology, Main risks = Revenue durability; churn; capitalised development; cash burn. Technology, Key ratios = ARR growth; churn; gross margin; EBITDA (dev costs expensed). Technology, Key documents = Customer cohort data; contracts; IP ownership. Technology, Stress scenario = Churn doubled; new bookings -30%. Technology, Security considerations = Few tangible assets; IP not lending value. Technology, Warning indicators = Rising churn; capitalised costs growing faster than revenue. Property, Main risks = Values, voids, interest rates, refinancing. Property, Key ratios = LTV; ICR; debt yield; WAULT. Property, Key documents = Valuation; tenancy schedule; leases. Property, Stress scenario = Section 32. Property, Security considerations = Section 32. Property, Warning indicators = Lease breaks; tenant distress; falling valuations. Recruitment, Main risks = Thin',\n",
       "  '30. Sector-Specific Underwriting\\n| - |\\n margins; compliance (IR35, umbrella companies); client concentration. Recruitment, Key ratios = Gross profit per placement; debtor days; PAYE compliance. Recruitment, Key documents = HMRC compliance evidence; client contracts. Recruitment, Stress scenario = Largest client lost; debtor days +20. Recruitment, Security considerations = Invoice finance; PAYE arrears rank ahead. Recruitment, Warning indicators = PAYE/VAT arrears; disputed invoices. Energy, Main risks = Transition risk; commodity prices; regulation; technology. Energy, Key ratios = EBITDA vs commodity price; contracted revenue %. Energy, Key documents = Offtake contracts; licences; transition plan. Energy, Stress scenario = Price -30%; carbon cost per scenario. Energy, Security considerations = Specialist assets; decommissioning liabilities. Energy, Warning indicators = Licence changes; stranded- asset indicators. Import / export, Main risks = FX; transit; sanctions; supplier and buyer default. Import / export, Key ratios = FX exposure % of costs/revenue; trade cycle days. Import / export, Key documents = Supplier/buyer contracts; hedging policy; shipping docs. Import / export, Stress scenario = GBP ±15%; shipment delays of 60 days. Import / export, Security considerations = Goods in transit; credit insurance assignment. Import / export, Warning indicators = Hedging lapses; new high-risk counterparties. E-commerce, Main risks = Customer acquisition cost; platform dependency; returns; inventory. E-commerce, Key ratios = Contribution margin after marketing; CAC payback; returns rate. E-commerce, Key documents = Platform reports; cohort data; marketing spend. E-commerce, Stress scenario = CAC +30%; returns +5pp. E-commerce, Security considerations = Stock (fashion: 80% haircut).',\n",
       "  '30. Sector-Specific Underwriting\\n| - |\\n E-commerce, Warning indicators = Marketplace account suspensions; rising CAC |\\n\\nUse of Sector Guides. Where a sector benchmark ratio and the general warning threshold in Section 9 differ, the stricter applies. Credit may not approve a facility that breaches a Sector Guide mandatory requirement without a Policy Exception.',\n",
       "  '42. Credit Application / Credit Memo Template\\nAll manually underwritten applications above L2 must use this template. Sections may be brief where a topic is immaterial, but none may be deleted; \"N/A\" must be justified in one line. The recommendation and key risks appear first so that the approver reads the conclusion before the evidence.',\n",
       "  \"42. Credit Application / Credit Memo Template\\n| - |\\n| 0, Section = Summary box. 0, Required content = Obligor; segment and overlays; TCE before/after; facilities requested; recommended decision; grade (model / final); PD; LGD; EL; RAROC; authority level required; exceptions (Y/N). 1, Section = Recommendation. 1, Required content = Approve / approve with amendments / decline, in one paragraph, with the main reasons. 2, Section = Customer. 2, Required content = Legal name, number, incorporation date, registered office, SIC, years trading, KYC status and Customer Risk Rating. 3, Section = Ownership. 3, Required content = Structure chart; shareholders and PSCs; UBOs; group connections and TCE. 4, Section = Management. 4, Required content = Directors' experience; succession; key-person risk; governance and reporting quality (Section 13.2 scores). 5, Section = Business model. 5, Required content = What the business does, who pays it, why customers stay; competitive position; customer and supplier concentration. 6, Section = Industry. 6, Required content = Northbridge sector classification and risk score; sector outlook; how the obligor differs from its sector. 7, Section = Existing banking relationship. 7, Required content = Facilities with Northbridge and others; account conduct (12m); utilisation; covenant compliance history; EWI history. 8, Section = Facilities requested. 8, Required content = For each: product, amount, tenor, repayment profile, pricing, fees. 9, Section = Purpose. 9, Required content = Use of funds; sources and uses table; owner contribution. 10, Section = Financial analysis. 10, Required content = 3-year spread with YTD; key ratios vs warning thresholds and sector benchmarks; trend narrative. 11, Section = Cash flow.\",\n",
       "  '42. Credit Application / Credit Memo Template\\n| - |\\n 11, Required content = Normalisation schedule; CFADS; historical pro-forma DSCR; bank- case forecast and assumptions vs management case |\\n',\n",
       "  '42. Credit Application / Credit Memo Template\\n| - |\\n| 12, Section = Leverage. 12, Required content = Senior and total debt / EBITDA (pre and post); debt capacity vs Section 15 limits. 13, Section = Security. 13, Required content = Security schedule: asset, ranking, MV, haircut, lending value, valuation date; security cover; unsecured exposure. 14, Section = Guarantees. 14, Required content = Guarantors, amounts, net worth, ILA status, or rationale for waiver. 15, Section = Risk grade. 15, Required content = Model score and grade; qualitative adjustments; overrides with reason codes. 16, Section = PD, LGD, EL. 16, Required content = Values used; pricing vs minimum margin; RAROC vs hurdle. 17, Section = Stress test. 17, Required content = Mild / moderate / severe results vs Section 14 criteria; sector stresses; runway; refinancing risk. 18, Section = Key risks. 18, Required content = The 3-6 risks that matter, each stated specifically. 19, Section = Mitigants. 19, Required content = Mitigant for each key risk, and residual risk. 20, Section = Covenants. 20, Required content = Financial (definition, level, testing, headroom vs bank case) and non- financial. 21, Section = Policy exceptions. 21, Required content = Each exception: rule, magnitude, justification, compensating factors. 22, Section = Conditions precedent. 22, Required content = Numbered list. 23, Section = Conditions subsequent. 23, Required content = Numbered list with due dates. 24, Section = Monitoring plan. 24, Required content = MI required, review frequency, EWIs specific to this obligor. 25, Section = ESG and climate. 25, Required content = Physical and transition assessment; insurance; actions. 26, Section',\n",
       "  '42. Credit Application / Credit Memo Template\\n| - |\\n = Independent challenge log. 26, Required content = Credit\\'s questions and the RM\\'s responses. 27, Section = Approval. 27, Required content = Approver(s), authority level, decision, amendments, conditions, date, next review date |\\n\\nQuality rules. No adjectives without evidence (\"strong management\" must be supported by Section 13 factors). All figures must reconcile to the spread. Every key risk must have a corresponding mitigant, covenant or monitoring action, or an explicit statement that it is accepted.',\n",
       "  'Outcome: the facilities as requested are declined, and an amended structure is approved.\\nThe request fails the mild and moderate stress tests in Section 14. A smaller term loan, funded alongside a £500k subordinated director loan, passes every test and is approved at Senior Credit Manager (L5) level. MGT Foods UK Ltd, its owners, customers and all figures are fictional.',\n",
       "  '43.1 Summary box\\nObligor, Detail = MGT Foods UK Ltd (fictional), private limited company, incorporated 2009. Business, Detail = Manufacturer of chilled and frozen ready meals and sauces for UK grocery retailers and food-service; single freehold site in the East Midlands; 85 employees. Segment / overlays, Detail = Medium (turnover £12.0m); owner-managed. Sector, Detail = Food manufacturing - Acceptable (Neutral); sector risk score 3. KYC, Detail = Complete; Customer Risk Rating Low. Requested, Detail = Term loan £2.0m (7 years) for a new production line; RCF £500k (3 years) replacing £300k overdraft. Existing Northbridge facilities, Detail = Term loan £600k outstanding (5 years remaining, fixed 6.50%); overdraft £300k limit (£150k drawn). Other lenders, Detail = Hire purchase £200k (vehicles), third-party asset finance company. TCE after (as recommended), Detail = £2.85m. Grade, Detail = Model score 640 → grade 6; final grade 6; Master Scale central PD 1.20%. LGD / EAD / EL, Detail = 28% / £2.71m / c.£9k p.a.. Recommendation, Detail = Decline as requested; approve amended structure (Section 43.11). Authority, Detail = L5 Senior Credit Manager, joint with Commercial Manager (TCE £2.85m, grade 6). Policy exceptions, Detail = None in the approved structure',\n",
       "  \"43.2 Ownership, management and business model\\nOwnership, Assessment = Managing Director and founder (age 61) 70%; Operations Director 15%; founder's sibling (non- executive, not employed) 15%. No corporate shareholders; PSC register verified\\nManagement, Assessment = MD: 30 years in food manufacturing. Operations Director: 12 years, joined 2018. Finance function: in- house financial controller, external accountants. Monthly management accounts produced within 20 days. No formal succession plan. Management score: Adequate (experience Strong; succession Weak). Business model, Assessment = Contract manufacture of own-label ready meals under supply agreements. Customer A (a national grocery retailer) 38% of revenue; top 3 customers 61%. Recurring weekly orders but supply agreements are 12- month rolling and terminable on 3 months' notice. Purpose of request, Assessment = Install a second high-speed production line (total cost £2.4m: equipment £2.0m, installation and commissioning £0.4m) to supply a new premium range to Customer A. A letter of intent has been received; the 3-year supply agreement is not yet signed. Management case, Assessment = New line adds £3.0m revenue and £450k EBITDA a year from FY28 (year to 31 March 2028)\",\n",
